<p>The U.S. Justice Department charged Sinaloa Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya and other current and former Mexican officials in an indictment unsealed Wednesday in New York. The indictment accuses them of drug trafficking, weapons offenses and helping the Sinaloa Cartel move large quantities of narcotics into the United States. It alleges the officials conspired with cartel leaders in exchange for bribes and political support, and abused public posts to protect cartel activity. President Claudia Sheinbaum and Mexican authorities said they would review U.S. materials for legal sufficiency before taking action, while Rocha denied wrongdoing.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/evvdYZgMpUZTxdw_KYjIWRrGiyliLRXaYYdkI85d7DY6o0LPyVdDqGEGvR_0qgocwbLIP9UsM5l3Rd7w3g9QYoAO6U-a2tWt38-_qv205k0SxZ7styIwNmyAKK0E3IumvfSzOAqc1JySFi64hzbKijQWTDGt4H-zwH8nWqWdHKj4hRxyMQPEo8KNy_bKh51l1y9Z7EmUCVCW4CHhRNDzy0XHbXHrnowNKkB119QF67RFD4hiqPdFo9igmkgT5fcxetWRssDeXZzJjEKcjw' alt='Sinaloa Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya speaks during a news conference in Mazatlán, Sinaloa state, Mexico, in April 2024.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Named defendants: Reports on the indictment also named Morena Sen. Enrique Inzunza, Culiacán Mayor Juan de Dios Gámez, Sinaloa deputy prosecutor Dámaso Castro and state finance official Enrique Díaz Vega.</li><li>Unusual case: AAJ News said the indictment of a sitting senior Mexican politician was extremely uncommon and expanded U.S. anti-cartel efforts beyond criminal kingpins to politicians.</li><li>Campaign allegation: La Jornada reported that the filing accused the defendants of helping secure Rocha’s election through direct intervention, including kidnappings and pressure on voters.</li><li>Possible penalties: The Rio Times reported that the defendants could face 40 years to life in prison if convicted on the charges.</li></ul><blockquote>Let these charges send a clear message to all officials around the globe who work with narco-traffickers: no matter your title or position, we are committed to bringing you to justice - Jay Clayton</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Claudia Sheinbaum: Sheinbaum said she would not allow outside governments to use the case for political purposes and said Mexico’s response would be handled by its own institutions. (<a href='https://www.military.com/mexico-to-investigate-us-indictment-of-officials-for-alleged-sinaloa-cartel-ties-sheinbaum-says'>Military.com</a>)</li><li>Rubén Rocha Moya: Rocha rejected the accusations on X, said they lacked truth or foundation, and called them an attack on the governing Morena movement. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330457706/us-accuses-mexican-governor-of-alleged-cartel-ties'>AAJ News</a>)</li><li>U.S. Drug Enforcement Administration: DEA Administrator Terrance Cole said officials accused in the case used positions of trust to shield cartel operations, turning corruption into a tool for violence and profit. (<a href='https://www.graphic.com.gh/international/international-news/us-charges-mexican-governor-and-other-leaders-with-aiding-drug-cartel.html'>Graphic Online</a>)</li><li>Crisis Group analyst David Mora: Mora told AFP that Rocha was very close to former President Andrés Manuel López Obrador, had been friends with him for decades and had campaigned with him. (<a href='https://www.nacion.com/el-mundo/gobernador-mexicano-acusado-de-narco-muy-cercano-a/AUQSHHPIORBMRJLWD6H42WX7YQ/story/'>La Nación</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-us-indictment-of-sitting-mexican-governor-puts-sheinbaum-in-a-bind/'>U.S. indictment of sitting Mexican governor puts Sheinbaum in a bind</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mexico-wants-evidence-officials-cartel-links-claudia-sheinbaum/'>Mexico wants evidence to back up U.S. drug charges against officials</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/30/us-charges-sinaloa-governor-and-other-